_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152716 :question/problem "Let $R=[0,1]\\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=5y-x+3.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186259532 :question/problem "Let $R=[-1,1]\\times [1,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=5x-3y+20.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186393480 :question/problem "Let $R=[1,4]\\times [1,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=4x-2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-27a8dfe2-6a96-5884-8d1c-c8b7f914c19a" :question/id #uuid "27a8dfe2-6a96-5884-8d1c-c8b7f914c19a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75352" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [2,9]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=11y-6x.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" :question/worked-solution "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=11y-6x$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=11y-6x$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/ff/ffa57d3e7fed08212f12d4b9d7c2e0a4d33a3aac71c8b8a03d1a9a7364484deb.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/c4/c4aa9e96fe72a8fb31ab62ff7eb0695cc64992bb9433be516461915a5c92baff.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j-1})=11{y}_{j-1}-6{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(11{y}_{j-1}-6{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $11{y}_{j-1}-6{x}_{i}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-730a83f8-2630-58ea-b90c-e36feb412b1d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-730a83f8-2630-58ea-b90c-e36feb412b1d" :answer-field/id #uuid "730a83f8-2630-58ea-b90c-e36feb412b1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4db0561e-8dcb-5a82-880f-8f39f966349f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-64ba2f62-940f-51e4-b382-4ba2a213b726" "answer-4db0561e-8dcb-5a82-880f-8f39f966349f" "answer-d3e8e717-81c6-5340-893f-4ae7e4c8fe45" "answer-c2a917a8-a750-512e-a24a-d5d184503876" "answer-138fc8d0-58bb-56a7-a4a1-0ead87eb84d2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64ba2f62-940f-51e4-b382-4ba2a213b726" :answer/id #uuid "64ba2f62-940f-51e4-b382-4ba2a213b726" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11{y}_{j-1}-6{x}_{i-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4db0561e-8dcb-5a82-880f-8f39f966349f" :answer/id #uuid "4db0561e-8dcb-5a82-880f-8f39f966349f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11{y}_{j-1}-6{x}_{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3e8e717-81c6-5340-893f-4ae7e4c8fe45" :answer/id #uuid "d3e8e717-81c6-5340-893f-4ae7e4c8fe45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11{y}_{j}-6{x}_{i-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2a917a8-a750-512e-a24a-d5d184503876" :answer/id #uuid "c2a917a8-a750-512e-a24a-d5d184503876" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9{y}_{j-1}+2{x}_{i-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-138fc8d0-58bb-56a7-a4a1-0ead87eb84d2" :answer/id #uuid "138fc8d0-58bb-56a7-a4a1-0ead87eb84d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9{y}_{j-1}+2{x}_{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c350d5bc-4404-57ee-b54b-285bbf9a8f0e" :question/id #uuid "c350d5bc-4404-57ee-b54b-285bbf9a8f0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-123516" :question/problem "Let $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=y-2x+10.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" :question/worked-solution "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=y-2x+10$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=y-2x+10$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/ff/ffa57d3e7fed08212f12d4b9d7c2e0a4d33a3aac71c8b8a03d1a9a7364484deb.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/c4/c4aa9e96fe72a8fb31ab62ff7eb0695cc64992bb9433be516461915a5c92baff.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j-1})={y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+10)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is ${y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+10.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5e3b32ab-28f5-5977-974b-8d287db52031"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5e3b32ab-28f5-5977-974b-8d287db52031" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e3b32ab-28f5-5977-974b-8d287db52031" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6468a315-f6fe-50ef-8ab8-9b9fb69c22ed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ab2fd54f-96ed-529e-86cd-ae8c068183d8" "answer-ca5808ed-e840-541e-a0a8-45d1856fb2d9" "answer-6468a315-f6fe-50ef-8ab8-9b9fb69c22ed" "answer-a63af55b-729d-5110-9d15-e2d5527d8efc" "answer-2f75718e-b688-57f7-8da7-5b80fb085343"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab2fd54f-96ed-529e-86cd-ae8c068183d8" :answer/id #uuid "ab2fd54f-96ed-529e-86cd-ae8c068183d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{y}_{j}-2{x}_{i-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca5808ed-e840-541e-a0a8-45d1856fb2d9" :answer/id #uuid "ca5808ed-e840-541e-a0a8-45d1856fb2d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i-1}+10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6468a315-f6fe-50ef-8ab8-9b9fb69c22ed" :answer/id #uuid "6468a315-f6fe-50ef-8ab8-9b9fb69c22ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a63af55b-729d-5110-9d15-e2d5527d8efc" :answer/id #uuid "a63af55b-729d-5110-9d15-e2d5527d8efc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{j-1}-{x}_{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f75718e-b688-57f7-8da7-5b80fb085343" :answer/id #uuid "2f75718e-b688-57f7-8da7-5b80fb085343" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{j}-2{x}_{i-1}+10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153351 :tutorial/content "We know how to write the lower Riemann sum for a function $f(x,y)$ over a *fixed* rectangular partition. In this lesson, we will learn how to write down an expression for the lower Riemann sum for an *arbitrary* rectangular partition. \n\nAs a concrete example, let $R = [0,1] \\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and define the function $f(x,y)$ as \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2+y^2.\n$$\n\nFurther, let $P$ be an arbitrary partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find an expression for $L(f, P),$ the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition. We proceed identically to the case where $P$ was fixed.\n\nFirst, we consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{j-1}$ and $y_j$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nSince we're computing the *lower* sum, we're interested in the *minimum* value of $f$ in this subregion.\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ increases as $x$ increases on $[0,1],$ and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ increases as $y$ increases on $[0,1].$\n\n![](images/67/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nTherefore, the *minimum* value of $f$ in the subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value of $f$ at the *bottom left corner* of the subregion.\n\n![](images/51/51808df96cf7dd295bfa24aa3641a601c821116e1a0014898ffe705036704a53.png)\n\nSo, if $m_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion, we have\n\n$$\nm_{ij} = f(x_{i-1}, y_{j-1}) = x_{i-1}^2 + y_{j-1}^2\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\leq i\\leq m$ and $1\\leq j\\leq n.$ \n\nSince the area of $R_{ij}$ is $\\Delta x_i\\Delta y_j,$ the volume of the rectangular solid with base $R_{ij}$ and height $m_{ij}$ is\n\n$$\nm_{ij}\\Delta x_i\\Delta y_j = (x_{i-1}^2 + y_{j-1}^2)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j \\, .\n$$\n\nFinally, summing up all of our rectangular solids over the entire partition $P$, we find that the lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n (x_{i-1}^2 + y_{j-1}^2)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j \\, .\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152327 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087757 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9880eb88-2023-5ce5-bda9-0c1fc8e76faf" "question-9165f710-3b4c-5eb0-a90d-63ff961aee99"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087573 :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=2+7\\textrm{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/da/da91da7425e6a02a202881eb1db3f1049d3f9cd204c0c1793c30e2d95676783b.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $0<      \\arg(z)<      \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ Further,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{7}{2})≈1.29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087574 :question/problem "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=-1+\\textrm{i}\\sqrt{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/1b/1bd35c5350e1dc1988cf98b8598a350701ef0b2a02a053b97ae2c89726de50c6.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}<        \\arg(z)<        \\pi.$ If $\\alpha$ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arg(z) = \\pi - \\dfrac\\pi 3 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087769 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b96d4599-55b6-52a4-b5e9-744fec2eb4d7" "question-cbe4274d-84e6-566b-a678-1dfe16c416af"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087589 :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-\\sqrt{3}-\\textrm{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/00/0095750158d9a296c9950c2d753ffe820735298c96cdf4de23aef0747862658d.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi<        \\arg(z)<        \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.$ If $\\alpha$ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arg(z) = \\pi +\\dfrac\\pi 6 = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087759 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-91c2517c-65da-5c92-ac2d-49406e18b196"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087575 :question/problem "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=1-\\textrm{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/5b/5b9ec84d8c3176c85c1b5ea546fc138231a129a11b906d786578616a1dd16cb1.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}<       \\arg(z)<       2\\pi.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha$ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha = \\arctan\\left(1\\right)  = \\dfrac\\pi 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arg(z) = 2\\pi - \\dfrac\\pi 4 =\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-9880eb88-2023-5ce5-bda9-0c1fc8e76faf" :question/id #uuid "9880eb88-2023-5ce5-bda9-0c1fc8e76faf" :question/math-academy-id "q-74660" :question/problem "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=4+4\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/fe/fe0207214fb16ac0871e15f3be7c9fa33670a7df3617f5f9e0b92b45a09c76af.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $0<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Further,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{4})=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-265d95ea-7f8d-5e15-8458-659dfbb603bf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-265d95ea-7f8d-5e15-8458-659dfbb603bf" :answer-field/id #uuid "265d95ea-7f8d-5e15-8458-659dfbb603bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b14f2b58-5f26-54f6-970e-19775cadeaf2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d69a3208-7d65-5d95-b5a7-de518fff3019" "answer-69993dcc-284a-5552-b290-2efb22ae5263" "answer-b14f2b58-5f26-54f6-970e-19775cadeaf2" "answer-92eaaa2e-7d8f-5f56-b19d-d506b27a8024" "answer-9c197951-1073-5f18-984d-9df842948758"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d69a3208-7d65-5d95-b5a7-de518fff3019" :answer/id #uuid "d69a3208-7d65-5d95-b5a7-de518fff3019" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69993dcc-284a-5552-b290-2efb22ae5263" :answer/id #uuid "69993dcc-284a-5552-b290-2efb22ae5263" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b14f2b58-5f26-54f6-970e-19775cadeaf2" :answer/id #uuid "b14f2b58-5f26-54f6-970e-19775cadeaf2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92eaaa2e-7d8f-5f56-b19d-d506b27a8024" :answer/id #uuid "92eaaa2e-7d8f-5f56-b19d-d506b27a8024" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c197951-1073-5f18-984d-9df842948758" :answer/id #uuid "9c197951-1073-5f18-984d-9df842948758" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9165f710-3b4c-5eb0-a90d-63ff961aee99" :question/id #uuid "9165f710-3b4c-5eb0-a90d-63ff961aee99" :question/math-academy-id "q-319490" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=5+12\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/c9/c94a6f3da0e2feb5421ad8652da0dde170d914ea4404a70c37a4112293fe810e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $0<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=arctan\\,(\\frac{12}{5})≈1.18,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-17a47526-4f69-5723-8a3e-3d9029c72ee7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-17a47526-4f69-5723-8a3e-3d9029c72ee7" :answer-field/id #uuid "17a47526-4f69-5723-8a3e-3d9029c72ee7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3e01582e-53ea-5a54-9f0c-d53b90245089" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3e01582e-53ea-5a54-9f0c-d53b90245089"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e01582e-53ea-5a54-9f0c-d53b90245089" :answer/id #uuid "3e01582e-53ea-5a54-9f0c-d53b90245089" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186233710 :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-4+6\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/32/325444dbc509efd27f6c5bf16c751b137532e2566a9dfaa69f92d9ce16bac51b.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{6}{4}) \\\\  & =0.982\\text{ }793…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi -0.982\\text{ }793…≈2.16,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351436 :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/37/373ea8254a9816f67bfbcaf993348f409c1047284d09c43e5d41e68b15c5af58.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\pi .$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.927\\,30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈\\pi -0.927\\,30≈2.21\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b96d4599-55b6-52a4-b5e9-744fec2eb4d7" :question/id #uuid "b96d4599-55b6-52a4-b5e9-744fec2eb4d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-319509" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-7-3\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/1d/1d131288e3d38767e1d82b68777e69667711e98665101093f3b7e544e9caea1e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan\\,(\\frac{3}{7})=0.404\\,892…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi +0.404\\,892…≈3.55,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-52688c42-4059-5864-ac3d-963accbc9ab7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-52688c42-4059-5864-ac3d-963accbc9ab7" :answer-field/id #uuid "52688c42-4059-5864-ac3d-963accbc9ab7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-20de621f-e8f8-5627-bd64-6ba71b41077b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-20de621f-e8f8-5627-bd64-6ba71b41077b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20de621f-e8f8-5627-bd64-6ba71b41077b" :answer/id #uuid "20de621f-e8f8-5627-bd64-6ba71b41077b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.55" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cbe4274d-84e6-566b-a678-1dfe16c416af" :question/id #uuid "cbe4274d-84e6-566b-a678-1dfe16c416af" :question/math-academy-id "q-8452" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-5-3\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/95/9596107dc58d6acb67f1014ebc17e632dd03173aa975fa84b196588db873d8e5.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})≈0.540\\,42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈\\pi +0.540\\,42≈3.68\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f010bca9-60ae-5099-b94b-38fd64086818"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f010bca9-60ae-5099-b94b-38fd64086818" :answer-field/id #uuid "f010bca9-60ae-5099-b94b-38fd64086818" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-25ee0bd4-ea00-578f-a776-d6fe24e71b44" :answer-field/choices ["answer-29f70e3d-7fb0-550c-8527-ffe9105791c5" "answer-3c35a209-2e4f-5ab8-8e85-b2749b02bdc9" "answer-97c8c749-bb23-5bf5-acab-f511bce8e986" "answer-003a02a8-0d57-518b-9d54-87c8ab14787c" "answer-25ee0bd4-ea00-578f-a776-d6fe24e71b44"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29f70e3d-7fb0-550c-8527-ffe9105791c5" :answer/id #uuid "29f70e3d-7fb0-550c-8527-ffe9105791c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c35a209-2e4f-5ab8-8e85-b2749b02bdc9" :answer/id #uuid "3c35a209-2e4f-5ab8-8e85-b2749b02bdc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97c8c749-bb23-5bf5-acab-f511bce8e986" :answer/id #uuid "97c8c749-bb23-5bf5-acab-f511bce8e986" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-003a02a8-0d57-518b-9d54-87c8ab14787c" :answer/id #uuid "003a02a8-0d57-518b-9d54-87c8ab14787c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.58" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25ee0bd4-ea00-578f-a776-d6fe24e71b44" :answer/id #uuid "25ee0bd4-ea00-578f-a776-d6fe24e71b44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.68" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186299780 :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=8\\sqrt{3}-8\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/78/78399e67e0ba69c1d7848a41aa5d4a5f8040fc578aab30a99f7e19ac86ece179.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{8}{8\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186298983 :answer-field/correct "answer-bf73cf84-e656-5869-bd56-89d900a17b12" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bf73cf84-e656-5869-bd56-89d900a17b12"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf73cf84-e656-5869-bd56-89d900a17b12" :answer/id #uuid "bf73cf84-e656-5869-bd56-89d900a17b12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-91c2517c-65da-5c92-ac2d-49406e18b196" :question/id #uuid "91c2517c-65da-5c92-ac2d-49406e18b196" :question/math-academy-id "q-319578" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=6-6\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/7c/7cdd04a6728120c66f4befafeb5a0bf2f053c8ed0ed2c1014e87549820e5c3ac.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{6}{6} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1bbcc34b-7cbd-525a-a9a2-b9f4d6626853"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1bbcc34b-7cbd-525a-a9a2-b9f4d6626853" :answer-field/id #uuid "1bbcc34b-7cbd-525a-a9a2-b9f4d6626853" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-82d788d2-536e-528f-b0c2-252148b7f7bf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-82d788d2-536e-528f-b0c2-252148b7f7bf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82d788d2-536e-528f-b0c2-252148b7f7bf" :answer/id #uuid "82d788d2-536e-528f-b0c2-252148b7f7bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088936 :tutorial/content "Consider the complex number $z=3+4\\textrm{i},$ as depicted on the Argand diagram below:\n\n![](images/c8/c857172451c97c24caab685a2f52097c0ca3c83df212c46a21f4a49c3edb19ca.png)\n\nThere is a natural angle $\\theta$ that $z$ makes with the positive real axis. This angle is called the **argument** of $z.$ We often denote the argument as $\\arg(z),$ or just $\\theta.$ The numeric value of $\\arg(z)$ is given in radians, and we restrict the values to \n\n$$\n0\\leq \\arg(z) \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nIn the triangle above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.93,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. Therefore $\\arg (z) \\approx 0.93$ to two decimal places.\n\n**Caution:** When you evaluate $\\arctan \\left( \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ on your calculator, be sure that you're in radians mode, and be sure to put parentheses around the $\\dfrac{4}{3}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087955 :activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-eaf43e73-9a1c-5cad-a4f5-266c54b6fab6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-eaf43e73-9a1c-5cad-a4f5-266c54b6fab6" :question/id #uuid "eaf43e73-9a1c-5cad-a4f5-266c54b6fab6" :question/math-academy-id "q-95408" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ must have all real eigenvalues\n\nII. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors, then $u⊥v$\n\nIII. $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable" :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal, but two eigenvectors corresponding to the same eigenvalue may not be orthogonal.\nThis can occur when the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvaule is greater than $1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c1086491-d596-5df5-9f5e-b792d5105671"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c1086491-d596-5df5-9f5e-b792d5105671" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1086491-d596-5df5-9f5e-b792d5105671" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f98d9e4b-b211-526a-8436-d0f20159b86b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f98d9e4b-b211-526a-8436-d0f20159b86b" "answer-5f102541-4e5b-562c-9b94-e08a3d1c19d7" "answer-49409b3e-6cf3-58d5-a30f-981d7b832aaa" "answer-884bd029-05ca-5b4a-a6a0-982c739c7b5d" "answer-c1d80d43-4b97-54e9-b8b5-55a67da752e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f98d9e4b-b211-526a-8436-d0f20159b86b" :answer/id #uuid "f98d9e4b-b211-526a-8436-d0f20159b86b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f102541-4e5b-562c-9b94-e08a3d1c19d7" :answer/id #uuid "5f102541-4e5b-562c-9b94-e08a3d1c19d7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49409b3e-6cf3-58d5-a30f-981d7b832aaa" :answer/id #uuid "49409b3e-6cf3-58d5-a30f-981d7b832aaa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-884bd029-05ca-5b4a-a6a0-982c739c7b5d" :answer/id #uuid "884bd029-05ca-5b4a-a6a0-982c739c7b5d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1d80d43-4b97-54e9-b8b5-55a67da752e6" :answer/id #uuid "c1d80d43-4b97-54e9-b8b5-55a67da752e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-0ee7b968-1996-5dc0-bce6-494a4e054d04" :activity/id #uuid "0ee7b968-1996-5dc0-bce6-494a4e054d04" :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049849 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-652a9dac-c9d8-5ab6-956b-06621fefc495" "step-5621b370-c250-590a-850c-a09cc9236f08" "step-33991c0d-45f8-5bde-a9e0-865b009ce6cf" "step-eab4f6a7-357a-59d5-b5d9-5cb4b9754a49" "step-204942ae-e32e-531e-8f95-9aa08d764a99"] :activity/first-step "step-652a9dac-c9d8-5ab6-956b-06621fefc495" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-652a9dac-c9d8-5ab6-956b-06621fefc495" :step/id #uuid "652a9dac-c9d8-5ab6-956b-06621fefc495" :step/content "question-eaf43e73-9a1c-5cad-a4f5-266c54b6fab6" :step/next "step-5621b370-c250-590a-850c-a09cc9236f08" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5621b370-c250-590a-850c-a09cc9236f08" :step/id #uuid "5621b370-c250-590a-850c-a09cc9236f08" :step/content 17592186378058 :step/next "step-33991c0d-45f8-5bde-a9e0-865b009ce6cf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-33991c0d-45f8-5bde-a9e0-865b009ce6cf" :step/id #uuid "33991c0d-45f8-5bde-a9e0-865b009ce6cf" :step/content 17592186395052 :step/next "step-eab4f6a7-357a-59d5-b5d9-5cb4b9754a49" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-eab4f6a7-357a-59d5-b5d9-5cb4b9754a49" :step/id #uuid "eab4f6a7-357a-59d5-b5d9-5cb4b9754a49" :step/content 17592186366684 :step/next "step-204942ae-e32e-531e-8f95-9aa08d764a99" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-204942ae-e32e-531e-8f95-9aa08d764a99" :step/id #uuid "204942ae-e32e-531e-8f95-9aa08d764a99" :step/content 17592186365052 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105909 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9ce41098-082c-5659-8a15-6d5205341359"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9ce41098-082c-5659-8a15-6d5205341359" :question/id #uuid "9ce41098-082c-5659-8a15-6d5205341359" :question/math-academy-id "q-73747" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(-1,1,1)$, $B(2,-2,1)$, $C(1,2,2)$, and $D(2,-1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i-2j+k)-(-i+j+k) \\\\  & =3i-3j \\\\  & =⟨3,-3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i+2j+2k)-(-i+j+k) \\\\  & =2i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(2i-j+4k)-(-i+j+k) \\\\  & =3i-2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|+0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(1\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 1)+3(2\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 1)+0 \\\\  & =15+9 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|24|=24.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-87f1cabe-8e50-570a-a56a-29d676db3c4b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-87f1cabe-8e50-570a-a56a-29d676db3c4b" :answer-field/id #uuid "87f1cabe-8e50-570a-a56a-29d676db3c4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e0f468c2-e5f3-55da-b91c-73e467a1b0d5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-36a59f0c-6350-5ee2-ab7b-1e2e0aaab216" "answer-1f4b5ae5-dd42-5130-a079-3437f957abdb" "answer-8dd330f2-c63f-5936-b6a4-80ea6b670b83" "answer-e0f468c2-e5f3-55da-b91c-73e467a1b0d5" "answer-e659aca7-3288-5e5d-8c4f-7e31771aa20e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36a59f0c-6350-5ee2-ab7b-1e2e0aaab216" :answer/id #uuid "36a59f0c-6350-5ee2-ab7b-1e2e0aaab216" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f4b5ae5-dd42-5130-a079-3437f957abdb" :answer/id #uuid "1f4b5ae5-dd42-5130-a079-3437f957abdb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8dd330f2-c63f-5936-b6a4-80ea6b670b83" :answer/id #uuid "8dd330f2-c63f-5936-b6a4-80ea6b670b83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0f468c2-e5f3-55da-b91c-73e467a1b0d5" :answer/id #uuid "e0f468c2-e5f3-55da-b91c-73e467a1b0d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e659aca7-3288-5e5d-8c4f-7e31771aa20e" :answer/id #uuid "e659aca7-3288-5e5d-8c4f-7e31771aa20e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105908 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-58e03782-f890-5578-a853-4a903526c725"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-58e03782-f890-5578-a853-4a903526c725" :question/id #uuid "58e03782-f890-5578-a853-4a903526c725" :question/math-academy-id "q-280718" :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨1,2,k⟩$, $b=⟨-3,0,4⟩$, and $c=⟨2,-1,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|+k\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0\\cdot 5-4\\cdot (-1))-2((-3)\\cdot 5-4\\cdot 2)+k((-3)\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =4-2(-15-8)+3k \\\\  & =50+3k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 50+3k & =0 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{50}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{50}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0d21ebbf-1121-50e2-8b37-00e70a6f94e2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0d21ebbf-1121-50e2-8b37-00e70a6f94e2" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d21ebbf-1121-50e2-8b37-00e70a6f94e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d62d2e68-5eee-5bc7-afa4-c823d3717d6e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d62d2e68-5eee-5bc7-afa4-c823d3717d6e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d62d2e68-5eee-5bc7-afa4-c823d3717d6e" :answer/id #uuid "d62d2e68-5eee-5bc7-afa4-c823d3717d6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{50}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-e5d016ea-97f9-5f7e-b3aa-b53ea168f095" :activity/id #uuid "e5d016ea-97f9-5f7e-b3aa-b53ea168f095" :activity/title "Volumes of Parallelepipeds" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049335 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-ff80067a-229b-58c3-ad50-df0a4ac2c88f" "step-7180c506-8977-5256-a014-4e1e049c02cf" "step-3e712f71-5132-5fc8-9220-1c4e12fc44db" "step-0d92669c-e2e2-524a-a056-94858ce6f069" "step-7c03e152-dc69-5f79-b833-63b638ead8ee"] :activity/first-step "step-ff80067a-229b-58c3-ad50-df0a4ac2c88f" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-ff80067a-229b-58c3-ad50-df0a4ac2c88f" :step/id #uuid "ff80067a-229b-58c3-ad50-df0a4ac2c88f" :step/content "question-9ce41098-082c-5659-8a15-6d5205341359" :step/next "step-7180c506-8977-5256-a014-4e1e049c02cf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7180c506-8977-5256-a014-4e1e049c02cf" :step/id #uuid "7180c506-8977-5256-a014-4e1e049c02cf" :step/content 17592186342081 :step/next "step-3e712f71-5132-5fc8-9220-1c4e12fc44db" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3e712f71-5132-5fc8-9220-1c4e12fc44db" :step/id #uuid "3e712f71-5132-5fc8-9220-1c4e12fc44db" :step/content 17592186459239 :step/next "step-0d92669c-e2e2-524a-a056-94858ce6f069" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0d92669c-e2e2-524a-a056-94858ce6f069" :step/id #uuid "0d92669c-e2e2-524a-a056-94858ce6f069" :step/content "question-58e03782-f890-5578-a853-4a903526c725" :step/next "step-7c03e152-dc69-5f79-b833-63b638ead8ee" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7c03e152-dc69-5f79-b833-63b638ead8ee" :step/id #uuid "7c03e152-dc69-5f79-b833-63b638ead8ee" :step/content 17592186295169 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186123188 :answer-field/choices 17592186123189] [:db/retract 17592186123188 :answer-field/choices 17592186123190] [:db/retract 17592186123188 :answer-field/choices 17592186123191] [:db/retract 17592186123188 :answer-field/choices 17592186123192] [:db/retract 17592186123188 :answer-field/choices 17592186123193] {:db/id 17592186125098 :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 5 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )^{2}-15) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-9{\\lambda }^{2}+9\\lambda -1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}-9{A}^{2}+9A-I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}-9{A}^{2}+9A-I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-9A+9I-{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =9I-9A+{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186123188 :answer-field/correct "answer-906cefe1-167f-55f1-a726-e75cd7b1f666" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ca77d26f-42a8-5ca7-858c-4a827ebda512" "answer-0894f3ea-f301-59cb-8cc0-94e15917c23c" "answer-9be942a4-212d-50e7-ad1b-4d20819d8e01" "answer-5bf1ffa6-1400-563e-9431-a2cb4ab16e21" "answer-906cefe1-167f-55f1-a726-e75cd7b1f666"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca77d26f-42a8-5ca7-858c-4a827ebda512" :answer/id #uuid "ca77d26f-42a8-5ca7-858c-4a827ebda512" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3I+9A-{A}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0894f3ea-f301-59cb-8cc0-94e15917c23c" :answer/id #uuid "0894f3ea-f301-59cb-8cc0-94e15917c23c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3I-9A+{A}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9be942a4-212d-50e7-ad1b-4d20819d8e01" :answer/id #uuid "9be942a4-212d-50e7-ad1b-4d20819d8e01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9I+3A-{A}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bf1ffa6-1400-563e-9431-a2cb4ab16e21" :answer/id #uuid "5bf1ffa6-1400-563e-9431-a2cb4ab16e21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9I+A+{A}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-906cefe1-167f-55f1-a726-e75cd7b1f666" :answer/id #uuid "906cefe1-167f-55f1-a726-e75cd7b1f666" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9I-9A+{A}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129190 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5502822c-28cd-5be6-838d-dcd2d5622d7d" "question-437b2f48-85ba-532a-9ac5-52a828cb51ba" "question-c3ba9c34-ab2d-5d40-bb57-ca2ed5ef7608"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5502822c-28cd-5be6-838d-dcd2d5622d7d" :question/id #uuid "5502822c-28cd-5be6-838d-dcd2d5622d7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-132566" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1}{\\int }_{{e}^{y-1}}^{3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/31/314b881b95ea804456b3e41a1d8fec70cf459e007e2120d9018e3bef02f1b523.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type II regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le x\\le 3\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1,\\,{e}^{y-1}\\le x\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx={e}^{y-1}\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c9/c9b097164e2b5215a051e18de055f3cce4c26600caf510bc3b0071b77b645089.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1}{\\int }_{{e}^{y-1}}^{3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e4f736f9-b340-574e-a7c8-fcb3f016514e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e4f736f9-b340-574e-a7c8-fcb3f016514e" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4f736f9-b340-574e-a7c8-fcb3f016514e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e9dc66d0-ea3e-5db7-9103-0b20306e7ab2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e9dc66d0-ea3e-5db7-9103-0b20306e7ab2" "answer-5c2c2bca-542a-586d-ad46-884f6485b4de" "answer-b5c3fbd3-d9cf-5d4e-a641-4c48e70eef96" "answer-42155f4f-8bc0-57e6-9490-3cff23b40646" "answer-774044da-9303-5308-898e-bc4f63b66972"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9dc66d0-ea3e-5db7-9103-0b20306e7ab2" :answer/id #uuid "e9dc66d0-ea3e-5db7-9103-0b20306e7ab2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c2c2bca-542a-586d-ad46-884f6485b4de" :answer/id #uuid "5c2c2bca-542a-586d-ad46-884f6485b4de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5c3fbd3-d9cf-5d4e-a641-4c48e70eef96" :answer/id #uuid "b5c3fbd3-d9cf-5d4e-a641-4c48e70eef96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42155f4f-8bc0-57e6-9490-3cff23b40646" :answer/id #uuid "42155f4f-8bc0-57e6-9490-3cff23b40646" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{1}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-774044da-9303-5308-898e-bc4f63b66972" :answer/id #uuid "774044da-9303-5308-898e-bc4f63b66972" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{1}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162025 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1e025671-c988-5444-b52c-2cc6ba70eb20"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1e025671-c988-5444-b52c-2cc6ba70eb20" :question/id #uuid "1e025671-c988-5444-b52c-2cc6ba70eb20" :question/math-academy-id "q-96576" :question/problem "The discrete random variable $X\\in Z$ can be approximated by the continuous random variable $Y.$ By applying an appropriate continuity correction, $P(X=-18)$ can be approximated as" :question/worked-solution "We construct an interval around $X=-18$ with a radius of $0.5:$\n\n![](images/9c/9ccbf2335f4db52e60760d4740ebf29ac054b6b249b6f0436e02fbff3311d7d4.png)\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=-18) & ≈P(-18-0.5\\le Y\\le -18+0.5) \\\\  & =P(-18.5\\le Y\\le -17.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4dfef436-f0dd-5029-95f9-3d6c9dc629aa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4dfef436-f0dd-5029-95f9-3d6c9dc629aa" :answer-field/id #uuid "4dfef436-f0dd-5029-95f9-3d6c9dc629aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1ce4674c-5083-5b36-98a7-b915e1a9a375" :answer-field/choices ["answer-87bf84ce-9d23-5a06-9b73-ed4ab7fc1913" "answer-1ce4674c-5083-5b36-98a7-b915e1a9a375" "answer-25557390-cc90-5458-a850-eaa5fdae3391" "answer-a3f9b909-0cca-5605-936d-c13ad832bc44" "answer-0d3acc1c-34d4-53a3-a2c2-3bd4a879f97f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87bf84ce-9d23-5a06-9b73-ed4ab7fc1913" :answer/id #uuid "87bf84ce-9d23-5a06-9b73-ed4ab7fc1913" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P(-18.5\\le Y\\le -17)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ce4674c-5083-5b36-98a7-b915e1a9a375" :answer/id #uuid "1ce4674c-5083-5b36-98a7-b915e1a9a375" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P(-18.5\\le Y\\le -17.5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25557390-cc90-5458-a850-eaa5fdae3391" :answer/id #uuid "25557390-cc90-5458-a850-eaa5fdae3391" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P(-18\\le Y\\le -17)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3f9b909-0cca-5605-936d-c13ad832bc44" :answer/id #uuid "a3f9b909-0cca-5605-936d-c13ad832bc44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P(-19\\le Y\\le -17)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d3acc1c-34d4-53a3-a2c2-3bd4a879f97f" :answer/id #uuid "0d3acc1c-34d4-53a3-a2c2-3bd4a879f97f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P(-19\\le Y\\le -17.5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4e15364c-186a-5328-8307-13438fe58309"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4e15364c-186a-5328-8307-13438fe58309" :question/id #uuid "4e15364c-186a-5328-8307-13438fe58309" :question/math-academy-id "q-130722" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})j \\\\  & =(2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2x)i+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)+2y)j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(2(1)(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({1}^{2})(0))-2(1))i+((1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({1}^{2})(0))+2(0))j \\\\  & =-2\\,i+j \\\\  & =⟨-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-2,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-2} & =\\frac{y-0}{1} \\\\ x-1 & =-2y \\\\ x+2y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9ced3481-0c5c-5eca-982f-047cab2301fc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9ced3481-0c5c-5eca-982f-047cab2301fc" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ced3481-0c5c-5eca-982f-047cab2301fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d617b5ac-9818-5c3b-ba03-76a6cc05aeca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-058f601c-efb8-52f4-ad80-c86475d38425" "answer-d617b5ac-9818-5c3b-ba03-76a6cc05aeca" "answer-ca4a37f5-406e-5615-bae5-8b7a2a141fbb" "answer-e3119854-8196-5825-a5c9-16cfb48b0d16" "answer-7f6d7d30-ea91-5f19-ad12-6c285ca3401e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-058f601c-efb8-52f4-ad80-c86475d38425" :answer/id #uuid "058f601c-efb8-52f4-ad80-c86475d38425" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x+3y-2=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d617b5ac-9818-5c3b-ba03-76a6cc05aeca" :answer/id #uuid "d617b5ac-9818-5c3b-ba03-76a6cc05aeca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+2y-1=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca4a37f5-406e-5615-bae5-8b7a2a141fbb" :answer/id #uuid "ca4a37f5-406e-5615-bae5-8b7a2a141fbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3119854-8196-5825-a5c9-16cfb48b0d16" :answer/id #uuid "e3119854-8196-5825-a5c9-16cfb48b0d16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-2y-1=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f6d7d30-ea91-5f19-ad12-6c285ca3401e" :answer/id #uuid "7f6d7d30-ea91-5f19-ad12-6c285ca3401e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-y-1=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172006 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e3898571-1060-5a54-af07-b5592b492d4f" "question-1455aec3-db48-5efe-b5bf-59b8688bf438" "question-eddfe563-295a-5298-9f24-7cdb158be9e9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e3898571-1060-5a54-af07-b5592b492d4f" :question/id #uuid "e3898571-1060-5a54-af07-b5592b492d4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-132552" :question/problem "On a busy waterway, barges pass by a particular point continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $10$ minutes. Determine the probability that the next barge will pass between $5$ and $7$ minutes after the previous one. Round your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since barges pass continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time between two successive barges using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time between two successive barges. Since a barge passes every $10$ minutes, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ barges per minute.\n\nThe time interval between two successive barges can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next barge will pass between $5$ and $7$ minutes after the previous one is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5<X<7) & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/10}{|}_{5}^{7} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-7/10}+{e}^{-5/10} \\\\  & ≈0.1099\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-74158e50-3525-5d26-bf97-113e74a7d386"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-74158e50-3525-5d26-bf97-113e74a7d386" :answer-field/id #uuid "74158e50-3525-5d26-bf97-113e74a7d386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e5302346-1455-507d-8c19-5af7fa10783e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e5302346-1455-507d-8c19-5af7fa10783e" "answer-eee1f799-c2f5-5fbc-b0b8-69720cd98bfb" "answer-02f44278-1870-59da-b693-ab5e9edbb7b4" "answer-59c37b7d-f2a8-5b03-96d5-27b94d5bddda" "answer-2fbb0c6a-76c4-5add-8d12-dd2f98f7de84"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5302346-1455-507d-8c19-5af7fa10783e" :answer/id #uuid "e5302346-1455-507d-8c19-5af7fa10783e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1099" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eee1f799-c2f5-5fbc-b0b8-69720cd98bfb" :answer/id #uuid "eee1f799-c2f5-5fbc-b0b8-69720cd98bfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1299" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02f44278-1870-59da-b693-ab5e9edbb7b4" :answer/id #uuid "02f44278-1870-59da-b693-ab5e9edbb7b4" :answer/type :answer.type/