2186155907 :answer-field/choices 17592186155910] [:db/retract 17592186155907 :answer-field/choices 17592186155911] [:db/retract 17592186155907 :answer-field/choices 17592186155912] [:db/retract 17592186155895 :answer-field/choices 17592186155896] [:db/retract 17592186155895 :answer-field/choices 17592186155897] [:db/retract 17592186155895 :answer-field/choices 17592186155898] [:db/retract 17592186155895 :answer-field/choices 17592186155899] [:db/retract 17592186155895 :answer-field/choices 17592186155900] [:db/retract 17592186155913 :answer-field/choices 17592186155914] [:db/retract 17592186155913 :answer-field/choices 17592186155915] [:db/retract 17592186155913 :answer-field/choices 17592186155916] [:db/retract 17592186155913 :answer-field/choices 17592186155917] [:db/retract 17592186155913 :answer-field/choices 17592186155918] {:db/id 17592186157295 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-623b63a4-baae-557b-86dd-761ba0b029fe" "question-f9ba8fdb-9397-5097-95f0-204a59ce4162" "question-0e2ee0d0-657b-565c-90c3-ae6c29ac6b1e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157099 :question/problem "![](images/3b/3b6739458c31d4ce752549f42083a66dc7a0a0914ac210837d0a041019d914c3.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sin\\left( 2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right)$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{-\\pi/8}^{3\\pi/8} \\sin^2\\left(2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind an integral that represents the total area bounded by the polar curve." :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2 \\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{-\\pi/8}^{3\\pi/8} \\sin^2\\left(2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\nA_{\\textrm{total}}=4\\cdot A = 2 \\int_{-\\pi/8}^{3\\pi/8} \\sin^2\\left(2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157296 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7f4b1de5-2584-553e-ad73-4df20f726215" "question-9182ab7c-62c2-510f-9183-c91606315f00" "question-b75e23ab-5c6f-5e0d-91f4-1cb8debf07f9" "question-950e52f2-cc86-53ef-af25-6a46ca25d518"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157100 :question/problem "Find the total area bounded by the polar curve $r=\\sin(7\\theta),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d9aa4ff23010048536fbcbadc9cff5dc2fc094895c00814dd9efccdc9750682a.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta$ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sin(7\\theta) = 0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $7\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n7\\theta = 0, \\pi \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{7}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{7}.$\n\n![](images/71/712d56708d2346f502a229c20596e7aa24ce4be13564e9461784c9114c55065a.png)\n\nSince there are $7$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =7\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14\\theta ))\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\frac{7}{4}(\\theta -\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(14\\theta ))|}_{0}^{\\pi /7} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{7}-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi ))-(0-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{7}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157297 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-43e3bf66-f5d8-57b0-9e72-5dc370f879ec" "question-8b33c7a8-87dc-53ea-9478-54f9a2f3e5e0" "question-50affdee-0a40-5255-b196-53de7a7824c6" "question-1ca5b3d9-2cf9-5f0b-b261-8040bf2d2224"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157101 :question/problem "![](images/e8/e89c77448446dccb3ececd6f05f1a2e410f18f5077e50c88144cd2101cec9d54.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=2\\cos\\left(3\\theta-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA = 2 \\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi/12}^{\\pi/4} \\cos^2\\left(3\\theta-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nBy changing the limits of integration, find an integral expression representing the total area bounded by the polar curve." :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA = 2 \\int_{-\\pi/12}^{\\pi/4} \\cos^2\\left(3\\theta-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ to $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ and the length of this interval is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} - \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n![](images/b9/b98a92acf42831b84597662f03865f0c5bdb831af0573206c56f33ee6f3528ae.svg)\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ to $\\theta = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{12}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nA_\\textrm{total} = 2 \\int_{-\\pi/12}^{11\\pi/12} \\cos^2\\left(3\\theta-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157828 :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd3af53e4d4f02c5978e8b4bc8508fb0455a0a09e7222377df68a2297bc30dae.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=4\\cdot A=8{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186155889 :answer-field/correct "answer-5227f4d9-586f-5a87-96f1-2d29829a5051" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e116c89d-0721-51ca-8376-6b9644097c9f" "answer-45626ab5-23ff-5b54-ab30-6536c8857200" "answer-5227f4d9-586f-5a87-96f1-2d29829a5051" "answer-53f41015-0e83-5163-bbaa-f1bb9ea38cb4" "answer-6d0b241e-150f-5225-9356-c59f965a081e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e116c89d-0721-51ca-8376-6b9644097c9f" :answer/id #uuid "e116c89d-0721-51ca-8376-6b9644097c9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45626ab5-23ff-5b54-ab30-6536c8857200" :answer/id #uuid "45626ab5-23ff-5b54-ab30-6536c8857200" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5227f4d9-586f-5a87-96f1-2d29829a5051" :answer/id #uuid "5227f4d9-586f-5a87-96f1-2d29829a5051" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53f41015-0e83-5163-bbaa-f1bb9ea38cb4" :answer/id #uuid "53f41015-0e83-5163-bbaa-f1bb9ea38cb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d0b241e-150f-5225-9356-c59f965a081e" :answer/id #uuid "6d0b241e-150f-5225-9356-c59f965a081e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-623b63a4-baae-557b-86dd-761ba0b029fe" :question/id #uuid "623b63a4-baae-557b-86dd-761ba0b029fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-124682" :question/problem "![](images/42/428af134250df6268b97998db880e33f88a6b5bd32859036e29e04fc1861e0c9.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $5$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/42/428af134250df6268b97998db880e33f88a6b5bd32859036e29e04fc1861e0c9.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=5\\cdot A=\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-835941de-1b21-50b1-bc5e-e054f925540b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-835941de-1b21-50b1-bc5e-e054f925540b" :answer-field/id #uuid "835941de-1b21-50b1-bc5e-e054f925540b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ffc359f4-c504-5c02-9fa5-ef8786493295" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8ac577e2-de00-593f-842f-889f13634e67" "answer-ffc359f4-c504-5c02-9fa5-ef8786493295" "answer-5450b792-cfd2-5428-85cb-6e721aad8477" "answer-b86b5b0e-81d1-54fb-8de3-463acc2dbc18" "answer-ec10ba12-c948-5373-9400-a4298e8eaf68"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ac577e2-de00-593f-842f-889f13634e67" :answer/id #uuid "8ac577e2-de00-593f-842f-889f13634e67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffc359f4-c504-5c02-9fa5-ef8786493295" :answer/id #uuid "ffc359f4-c504-5c02-9fa5-ef8786493295" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5450b792-cfd2-5428-85cb-6e721aad8477" :answer/id #uuid "5450b792-cfd2-5428-85cb-6e721aad8477" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b86b5b0e-81d1-54fb-8de3-463acc2dbc18" :answer/id #uuid "b86b5b0e-81d1-54fb-8de3-463acc2dbc18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec10ba12-c948-5373-9400-a4298e8eaf68" :answer/id #uuid "ec10ba12-c948-5373-9400-a4298e8eaf68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f9ba8fdb-9397-5097-95f0-204a59ce4162" :question/id #uuid "f9ba8fdb-9397-5097-95f0-204a59ce4162" :question/math-academy-id "q-124684" :question/problem "![](images/a0/a0ec6bc2ecc45a67d2a6340039eee6cbb090ed7d15776e75b94a6c6d910a86e5.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $5$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/a0/a0ec6bc2ecc45a67d2a6340039eee6cbb090ed7d15776e75b94a6c6d910a86e5.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=5\\cdot A=\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0c411aaa-ddaa-5464-8abb-dd307e74030d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0c411aaa-ddaa-5464-8abb-dd307e74030d" :answer-field/id #uuid "0c411aaa-ddaa-5464-8abb-dd307e74030d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e7711dd9-3cc9-51a7-ba89-7107451ca153" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c4b7463f-7f4d-5f03-a9e1-2942e0699bb3" "answer-25444643-934c-52ab-b890-db2ee6187d6e" "answer-f2c3f927-4c06-5422-a227-69b04f7920cc" "answer-e7711dd9-3cc9-51a7-ba89-7107451ca153" "answer-f6289bae-3a41-5c9e-8f1d-1c68a61a70d5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4b7463f-7f4d-5f03-a9e1-2942e0699bb3" :answer/id #uuid "c4b7463f-7f4d-5f03-a9e1-2942e0699bb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25444643-934c-52ab-b890-db2ee6187d6e" :answer/id #uuid "25444643-934c-52ab-b890-db2ee6187d6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2c3f927-4c06-5422-a227-69b04f7920cc" :answer/id #uuid "f2c3f927-4c06-5422-a227-69b04f7920cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7711dd9-3cc9-51a7-ba89-7107451ca153" :answer/id #uuid "e7711dd9-3cc9-51a7-ba89-7107451ca153" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6289bae-3a41-5c9e-8f1d-1c68a61a70d5" :answer/id #uuid "f6289bae-3a41-5c9e-8f1d-1c68a61a70d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0e2ee0d0-657b-565c-90c3-ae6c29ac6b1e" :question/id #uuid "0e2ee0d0-657b-565c-90c3-ae6c29ac6b1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-83516" :question/problem "![](images/14/140947c29667712d4a1071305396fff56a24f0a5c11f26e31368b8cc011f2863.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$ is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 4=8$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=8\\cdot A=12{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-72c60208-16e5-5779-9065-2feb76aee1f9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-72c60208-16e5-5779-9065-2feb76aee1f9" :answer-field/id #uuid "72c60208-16e5-5779-9065-2feb76aee1f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b26a6ab9-c41c-52e2-907b-4ffdb43b0d2b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b26a6ab9-c41c-52e2-907b-4ffdb43b0d2b" "answer-3eedec8a-d18b-5b88-83c4-00e34bb3b972" "answer-1efbdd8f-088c-54e2-94f2-33585aa4d9c8" "answer-f2a6b41e-820d-56bf-ae6b-fd6bae64c165" "answer-fc8fb72b-e66d-5acb-a71a-2e252f1c84fa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b26a6ab9-c41c-52e2-907b-4ffdb43b0d2b" :answer/id #uuid "b26a6ab9-c41c-52e2-907b-4ffdb43b0d2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3eedec8a-d18b-5b88-83c4-00e34bb3b972" :answer/id #uuid "3eedec8a-d18b-5b88-83c4-00e34bb3b972" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1efbdd8f-088c-54e2-94f2-33585aa4d9c8" :answer/id #uuid "1efbdd8f-088c-54e2-94f2-33585aa4d9c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2a6b41e-820d-56bf-ae6b-fd6bae64c165" :answer/id #uuid "f2a6b41e-820d-56bf-ae6b-fd6bae64c165" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc8fb72b-e66d-5acb-a71a-2e252f1c84fa" :answer/id #uuid "fc8fb72b-e66d-5acb-a71a-2e252f1c84fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157831 :question/problem "![](images/09/09bc06001905956e32a4ff8cfff25a875a6933e19c365b2f9b159d8711a05048.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/09/09bc06001905956e32a4ff8cfff25a875a6933e19c365b2f9b159d8711a05048.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=3\\cdot A=\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186155907 :answer-field/correct "answer-f1473918-1ba6-5e3e-bb10-5a77e78b1483" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0ebc643b-565a-59ad-a7f3-6eb56c2a2256" "answer-604b7cdb-42b3-51c3-b697-9adc283c8c74" "answer-f1473918-1ba6-5e3e-bb10-5a77e78b1483" "answer-c0242d69-b07a-5c78-ba50-a2001826e357" "answer-1279ff3f-5245-521f-ba84-156f57eb4ff4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ebc643b-565a-59ad-a7f3-6eb56c2a2256" :answer/id #uuid "0ebc643b-565a-59ad-a7f3-6eb56c2a2256" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-604b7cdb-42b3-51c3-b697-9adc283c8c74" :answer/id #uuid "604b7cdb-42b3-51c3-b697-9adc283c8c74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{3\\pi /10}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1473918-1ba6-5e3e-bb10-5a77e78b1483" :answer/id #uuid "f1473918-1ba6-5e3e-bb10-5a77e78b1483" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0242d69-b07a-5c78-ba50-a2001826e357" :answer/id #uuid "c0242d69-b07a-5c78-ba50-a2001826e357" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1279ff3f-5245-521f-ba84-156f57eb4ff4" :answer/id #uuid "1279ff3f-5245-521f-ba84-156f57eb4ff4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{5\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7f4b1de5-2584-553e-ad73-4df20f726215" :question/id #uuid "7f4b1de5-2584-553e-ad73-4df20f726215" :question/math-academy-id "q-351293" :question/problem "![](images/c8/c8afd0a30a2796cc1590e97c8d71e1d47128ac1993d2875072877149631642e5.png)\n\nFind the total area ${A}_{\\text{total}}$ bounded by the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ),$ shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{total}}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $-\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{10}.$\n\n![](images/ca/caacb78b0f37dcc58d6568a571c99bc569c7eb95aeacf724d5df33e35ba21fa7.png)\n\nSince there are $5$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =5\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{\\pi }{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d7ca41cb-2481-5f25-ab16-b237d166c5ff"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d7ca41cb-2481-5f25-ab16-b237d166c5ff" :answer-field/id #uuid "d7ca41cb-2481-5f25-ab16-b237d166c5ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-788347b9-f328-50bb-9f8d-44dd99b00eb6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-788347b9-f328-50bb-9f8d-44dd99b00eb6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-788347b9-f328-50bb-9f8d-44dd99b00eb6" :answer/id #uuid "788347b9-f328-50bb-9f8d-44dd99b00eb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9182ab7c-62c2-510f-9183-c91606315f00" :question/id #uuid "9182ab7c-62c2-510f-9183-c91606315f00" :question/math-academy-id "q-125329" :question/problem "![](images/94/94a5da0925a82b91d345fa7811e41c219c19ca48d76b30927e920efed3b2edb3.png)\n\nFind the total area bounded by the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ),$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $6\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n6\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{12}.$\n\n![](images/7f/7f1a9ba1b4d9a86e23b8634dedaf6d4e1a651efbe4fc7aa27ac4dbb5d443018f.png)\n\nSince there are $12$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =12\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =12\\cdot 8{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =48{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12} \\\\  & =48[(\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =48(\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-38f2fe27-3752-50bd-8e74-45d47d4357a8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-38f2fe27-3752-50bd-8e74-45d47d4357a8" :answer-field/id #uuid "38f2fe27-3752-50bd-8e74-45d47d4357a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1b33f10d-c2bd-51b2-9314-14748e5b22b4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1fd2fcc1-904f-5624-a891-21600d6a3559" "answer-1b33f10d-c2bd-51b2-9314-14748e5b22b4" "answer-319af86b-63e6-5b3a-8f84-af301c7e96fa" "answer-5de93751-8e37-5c0e-ade7-b00a0286b1d9" "answer-b62a2b7d-1d9a-5e11-8aa5-b6a9b07a1289"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fd2fcc1-904f-5624-a891-21600d6a3559" :answer/id #uuid "1fd2fcc1-904f-5624-a891-21600d6a3559" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b33f10d-c2bd-51b2-9314-14748e5b22b4" :answer/id #uuid "1b33f10d-c2bd-51b2-9314-14748e5b22b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-319af86b-63e6-5b3a-8f84-af301c7e96fa" :answer/id #uuid "319af86b-63e6-5b3a-8f84-af301c7e96fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5de93751-8e37-5c0e-ade7-b00a0286b1d9" :answer/id #uuid "5de93751-8e37-5c0e-ade7-b00a0286b1d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b62a2b7d-1d9a-5e11-8aa5-b6a9b07a1289" :answer/id #uuid "b62a2b7d-1d9a-5e11-8aa5-b6a9b07a1289" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b75e23ab-5c6f-5e0d-91f4-1cb8debf07f9" :question/id #uuid "b75e23ab-5c6f-5e0d-91f4-1cb8debf07f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-351400" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcec4003e842d279dbed89307ef84a230b16443fe515a8cc621aea72bf8829d3.png)\n\nFind the total area ${A}_{\\text{total}}$ bounded by the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ),$ shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{total}}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{5}.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c35b0e3c4e51b164097e6deb11e4418726927f478f934e41d1250388e9a27e5.png)\n\nSince there are $5$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =5\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{16}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =40\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =20{(\\theta -\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\theta ))|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =20[(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi ))-(0-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =20\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be158308-89e7-524f-868f-9564cb669864"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be158308-89e7-524f-868f-9564cb669864" :answer-field/id #uuid "be158308-89e7-524f-868f-9564cb669864" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-043e78d4-5f01-563f-8dd0-f38020b32e28" :answer-field/choices ["answer-043e78d4-5f01-563f-8dd0-f38020b32e28"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-043e78d4-5f01-563f-8dd0-f38020b32e28" :answer/id #uuid "043e78d4-5f01-563f-8dd0-f38020b32e28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-950e52f2-cc86-53ef-af25-6a46ca25d518" :question/id #uuid "950e52f2-cc86-53ef-af25-6a46ca25d518" :question/math-academy-id "q-351393" :question/problem "![](images/21/219480e656f2f5913d7a83d93871c09b334600365d46a041ae63607d3f2f8326.png)\n\nFind the total area ${A}_{\\text{total}}$ bounded by the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),$ shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{total}}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $4\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n4\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\n![](images/e1/e1f3651f2c120b65063015a17a7e657ccb5b2e87df475660785d35748c05557a.png)\n\nSince there are $8$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =8\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{16}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =64\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =32[(\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =32(\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-972f7a9a-9438-5af7-a972-1e1761110b8a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-972f7a9a-9438-5af7-a972-1e1761110b8a" :answer-field/id #uuid "972f7a9a-9438-5af7-a972-1e1761110b8a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-88a984d6-e24d-5ad2-9d64-1bf9b0d07a93" :answer-field/choices ["answer-88a984d6-e24d-5ad2-9d64-1bf9b0d07a93"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88a984d6-e24d-5ad2-9d64-1bf9b0d07a93" :answer/id #uuid "88a984d6-e24d-5ad2-9d64-1bf9b0d07a93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157829 :question/problem "![](images/47/47eb2f1073f62129ba2fbf7ae154b9733ff2d5bf09af30053f7b708d7a8d21b0.png)\n\nFind the total area bounded by the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n2\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/22/220bd5e2559f7e3ef483b1315d1dd98771cf81d8d181a161b87d8084773259e6.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =4\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={(\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186155895 :answer-field/correct "answer-1ca96453-2812-54b1-ac9f-2bc70296b399" :answer-field/choices ["answer-527f5534-b9ff-58e8-814b-8cf2eb2ab2d8" "answer-5f450753-49cb-5b4a-a620-d8dc65d78d55" "answer-1ca96453-2812-54b1-ac9f-2bc70296b399" "answer-a418680f-7ad8-563a-ace3-88e406955935" "answer-01a603b1-e7b9-5111-9287-7aa72828eb6e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-527f5534-b9ff-58e8-814b-8cf2eb2ab2d8" :answer/id #uuid "527f5534-b9ff-58e8-814b-8cf2eb2ab2d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f450753-49cb-5b4a-a620-d8dc65d78d55" :answer/id #uuid "5f450753-49cb-5b4a-a620-d8dc65d78d55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ca96453-2812-54b1-ac9f-2bc70296b399" :answer/id #uuid "1ca96453-2812-54b1-ac9f-2bc70296b399" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a418680f-7ad8-563a-ace3-88e406955935" :answer/id #uuid "a418680f-7ad8-563a-ace3-88e406955935" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01a603b1-e7b9-5111-9287-7aa72828eb6e" :answer/id #uuid "01a603b1-e7b9-5111-9287-7aa72828eb6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-43e3bf66-f5d8-57b0-9e72-5dc370f879ec" :question/id #uuid "43e3bf66-f5d8-57b0-9e72-5dc370f879ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-83484" :question/problem "![](images/34/34e3bbdee3f70fe0111574f0d0fab772acf98de668e2eeb72464e6611a1fabcd.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/34/34e3bbdee3f70fe0111574f0d0fab772acf98de668e2eeb72464e6611a1fabcd.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3c945d12-5ddf-5d29-b1ff-6f3e26849c7d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3c945d12-5ddf-5d29-b1ff-6f3e26849c7d" :answer-field/id #uuid "3c945d12-5ddf-5d29-b1ff-6f3e26849c7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f2b98e86-56c1-5a75-b49c-7e744474633d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ebc61141-892a-5b47-bb30-ac12b2c0629b" "answer-b2eef91a-4bda-57e9-8dc9-d19c65f2191b" "answer-e8c8d26d-0f5e-5aa7-80ac-7d5f0bd84561" "answer-f2b98e86-56c1-5a75-b49c-7e744474633d" "answer-6eb18d81-1f63-599d-9330-be80543a661b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebc61141-892a-5b47-bb30-ac12b2c0629b" :answer/id #uuid "ebc61141-892a-5b47-bb30-ac12b2c0629b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{5\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2eef91a-4bda-57e9-8dc9-d19c65f2191b" :answer/id #uuid "b2eef91a-4bda-57e9-8dc9-d19c65f2191b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8c8d26d-0f5e-5aa7-80ac-7d5f0bd84561" :answer/id #uuid "e8c8d26d-0f5e-5aa7-80ac-7d5f0bd84561" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{5\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2b98e86-56c1-5a75-b49c-7e744474633d" :answer/id #uuid "f2b98e86-56c1-5a75-b49c-7e744474633d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6eb18d81-1f63-599d-9330-be80543a661b" :answer/id #uuid "6eb18d81-1f63-599d-9330-be80543a661b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8b33c7a8-87dc-53ea-9478-54f9a2f3e5e0" :question/id #uuid "8b33c7a8-87dc-53ea-9478-54f9a2f3e5e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-83485" :question/problem "![](images/85/85730909004322c2c8b6d065ff1cdb4b0662830007bd5aa14c8c8c4bda798206.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/85/85730909004322c2c8b6d065ff1cdb4b0662830007bd5aa14c8c8c4bda798206.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\pi ]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =0$ to $\\theta =\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e45758eb-810c-5093-acfa-015e3b2ab053"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e45758eb-810c-5093-acfa-015e3b2ab053" :answer-field/id #uuid "e45758eb-810c-5093-acfa-015e3b2ab053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0a2cc0dc-e2e1-5b85-87ec-47df41d520a4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f8f5048c-ee98-5951-88c7-66b4a1c08544" "answer-3f16e717-df6e-5066-9901-a556dc9ad746" "answer-f332fbbe-56f7-5d0e-a468-4f968b7b69ac" "answer-0a2cc0dc-e2e1-5b85-87ec-47df41d520a4" "answer-a55f7afa-35a1-5941-9320-ab16f063a2e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8f5048c-ee98-5951-88c7-66b4a1c08544" :answer/id #uuid "f8f5048c-ee98-5951-88c7-66b4a1c08544" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f16e717-df6e-5066-9901-a556dc9ad746" :answer/id #uuid "3f16e717-df6e-5066-9901-a556dc9ad746" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f332fbbe-56f7-5d0e-a468-4f968b7b69ac" :answer/id #uuid "f332fbbe-56f7-5d0e-a468-4f968b7b69ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a2cc0dc-e2e1-5b85-87ec-47df41d520a4" :answer/id #uuid "0a2cc0dc-e2e1-5b85-87ec-47df41d520a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a55f7afa-35a1-5941-9320-ab16f063a2e6" :answer/id #uuid "a55f7afa-35a1-5941-9320-ab16f063a2e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157832 :question/problem "![](images/3a/3aa82e021a1871bd17abfd72570452bc6aefe358a8fddd3cb8e92bb6ff91c5a9.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/3a/3aa82e021a1871bd17abfd72570452bc6aefe358a8fddd3cb8e92bb6ff91c5a9.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $4$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,{{[\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}]}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{11\\pi /6}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186155913 :answer-field/correct "answer-b9a93243-035f-54e5-a2ed-ba2740b32501" :answer-field/choices ["answer-567b1d6d-20c9-5e7e-ad5c-e9bbd06e3774" "answer-1ff8ea5a-73f9-53b1-aa2b-d89a1b062fc3" "answer-b9a93243-035f-54e5-a2ed-ba2740b32501" "answer-f5bc3e69-6223-5ab4-87cd-e8f77504d1c1" "answer-df677947-f1f2-54d0-b9ed-85515727e9d1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-567b1d6d-20c9-5e7e-ad5c-e9bbd06e3774" :answer/id #uuid "567b1d6d-20c9-5e7e-ad5c-e9bbd06e3774" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ff8ea5a-73f9-53b1-aa2b-d89a1b062fc3" :answer/id #uuid "1ff8ea5a-73f9-53b1-aa2b-d89a1b062fc3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{11\\pi /6}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9a93243-035f-54e5-a2ed-ba2740b32501" :answer/id #uuid "b9a93243-035f-54e5-a2ed-ba2740b32501" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{11\\pi /6}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5bc3e69-6223-5ab4-87cd-e8f77504d1c1" :answer/id #uuid "f5bc3e69-6223-5ab4-87cd-e8f77504d1c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{4\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df677947-f1f2-54d0-b9ed-85515727e9d1" :answer/id #uuid "df677947-f1f2-54d0-b9ed-85515727e9d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-50affdee-0a40-5255-b196-53de7a7824c6" :question/id #uuid "50affdee-0a40-5255-b196-53de7a7824c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-125863" :question/problem "![](images/66/66616f493d84804ec9c7c2a1e3b8598404f1f57ffda2e0a68718342f508010af.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $7$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=0,$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0 \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the fifth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the fourth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{9} & ={\\theta }_{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{10} & ={\\theta }_{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the interval for the sixth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the fifth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{11} & ={\\theta }_{9}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{12} & ={\\theta }_{10}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the seventh petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the sixth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{13} & ={\\theta }_{11}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{14} & ={\\theta }_{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $7$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[-\\frac{\\pi }{7},0]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{7},\\frac{2\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,{{[\\frac{2\\pi }{7},\\frac{3\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{th}},\\,{{[\\frac{3\\pi }{7},\\frac{4\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{5\\text{th}}\\,{{[\\frac{4\\pi }{7},\\frac{5\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{6\\text{th}},\\,{{[\\frac{5\\pi }{7},\\frac{6\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{7\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7cf4a6ed-16c7-5aba-80c0-63cd22eaebcb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7cf4a6ed-16c7-5aba-80c0-63cd22eaebcb" :answer-field/id #uuid "7cf4a6ed-16c7-5aba-80c0-63cd22eaebcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-831d59c7-dc3e-55d3-969a-79579b8a2e00" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1747e57c-4ef9-5157-a45d-bc4f36bf748e" "answer-dacb2b57-d53d-5242-94a7-23fcf1973ca7" "answer-831d59c7-dc3e-55d3-969a-79579b8a2e00" "answer-00e7683d-837d-5f5c-88b4-9f43a2d9f138" "answer-4bb3e12e-9656-5b69-bd45-2db95263545f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1747e57c-4ef9-5157-a45d-bc4f36bf748e" :answer/id #uuid "1747e57c-4ef9-5157-a45d-bc4f36bf748e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta -\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dacb2b57-d53d-5242-94a7-23fcf1973ca7" :answer/id #uuid "dacb2b57-d53d-5242-94a7-23fcf1973ca7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /14}^{3\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-831d59c7-dc3e-55d3-969a-79579b8a2e00" :answer/id #uuid "831d59c7-dc3e-55d3-969a-79579b8a2e00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00e7683d-837d-5f5c-88b4-9f43a2d9f138" :answer/id #uuid "00e7683d-837d-5f5c-88b4-9f43a2d9f138" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bb3e12e-9656-5b69-bd45-2db95263545f" :answer/id #uuid "4bb3e12e-9656-5b69-bd45-2db95263545f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta -\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1ca5b3d9-2cf9-5f0b-b261-8040bf2d2224" :question/id #uuid "1ca5b3d9-2cf9-5f0b-b261-8040bf2d2224" :question/math-academy-id "q-125695" :question/problem "![](images/17/172737d1a5b28b9a60958dc7e6b1748db1912021b7df4ad26045648063037fd8.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{13\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $4$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[-\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{5\\pi }{8}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{5\\pi }{8},\\frac{9\\pi }{8}]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,{{[\\frac{9\\pi }{8},\\frac{13\\pi }{8}]}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{8}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f3f71d5c-aae5-521c-b9e4-78cdc0efc697"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f3f71d5c-aae5-521c-b9e4-78cdc0efc697" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f71d5c-aae5-521c-b9e4-78cdc0efc697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-640c8082-9d89-5f36-b453-20aa27bdcf44" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4c27fd42-8cff-578f-b7b7-132d0e72f699" "answer-ee2ff5b2-7d3f-5bc8-8b07-edf54d53bb3c" "answer-3bdda5ba-c7b8-5b1e-8e9a-e2ebee5f3bc5" "answer-640c8082-9d89-5f36-b453-20aa27bdcf44" "answer-45b57772-1a24-517a-95cc-031e14b7b047"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c27fd42-8cff-578f-b7b7-132d0e72f699" :answer/id #uuid "4c27fd42-8cff-578f-b7b7-132d0e72f699" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee2ff5b2-7d3f-5bc8-8b07-edf54d53bb3c" :answer/id #uuid "ee2ff5b2-7d3f-5bc8-8b07-edf54d53bb3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bdda5ba-c7b8-5b1e-8e9a-e2ebee5f3bc5" :answer/id #uuid "3bdda5ba-c7b8-5b1e-8e9a-e2ebee5f3bc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{11\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-640c8082-9d89-5f36-b453-20aa27bdcf44" :answer/id #uuid "640c8082-9d89-5f36-b453-20aa27bdcf44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45b57772-1a24-517a-95cc-031e14b7b047" :answer/id #uuid "45b57772-1a24-517a-95cc-031e14b7b047" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{9\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta -\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158484 :tutorial/content "![](images/34/34fdc5325fe4b1fb73d203ccb9db81e5d8b4b1cb02b3efda2b928addc2e458e6.png)\n\nSuppose that the area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sin(5\\theta)$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi/5} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the **total area** bounded by the curve? \n\nOne method is to count the number of petals and multiply the area of one petal by the total number of petals. In our case, we have $5$ petals in total, and so the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\nA_\\textrm{total}=5A =  \\dfrac{5}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi/5} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158485 :tutorial/content "![](images/34/34fdc5325fe4b1fb73d203ccb9db81e5d8b4b1cb02b3efda2b928addc2e458e6.png)\n\nLet's go back to our polar curve $r=\\sin(5\\theta).$ Recall that the area bounded by one petal is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi/5} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nCan we find an expression for the **total area** bounded by the curve by varying the limits of integration only?\n\nIndeed, we can, and we do this by extending the upper bound of the integral to include all $5$ loops. \n\nNote that:\n\n- The $1$st petal is traversed from $\\theta_1=0$ to $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{5},$ and the length of this interval is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}.$\n\n- To find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{5} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{2\\pi }{5} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nContinuing this method, we obtain the following intervals for all $5$ petals of our rose:\n\n![](images/81/814d2ff9a4ca7e8c7e6d85cc374eff37034e541338705c2abf621e5fd82f90e0.png)\n\n![](images/6c/6cc39d6b6fdf29c3b582f867ea9e769b5c6bf466bc57d53a3f346e092375b89c.svg)\n\nNote that the curve traces the petals out of order. However, all $5$ petals are traced out as $\\theta$ ranges from $0$ to $\\pi.$\n\nTherefore, we can obtain the total area of the given polar curve if we traverse it from $\\theta = 0$ to $\\theta = \\pi \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nA_\\textrm{total} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** It might be tempting to extend the top bound to $2\\pi.$ However, the entire curve is traced out on the interval $\\theta\\in [0,\\pi],$ so integrating from $0$ to $2\\pi$ would give us *twice* the total area bounded by the curve!" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157489 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110593 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4dbd2fd5-279c-5b4f-83af-6acbee35f908" "question-07d6770c-e373-5366-b946-966ce2aa6000"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110494 :question/problem "Find the exact solution of the equation $2^{x+1}=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take $\\log_2$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+1} & =3 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x+1}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\log_b \\left( b^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left( 3 \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ x & ={log}_{2}⁡(3)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x= \\log_2 \\left( 3 \\right) - 1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110594 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d436286b-8ea5-5b92-b174-4ebb9b14bf53" "question-918c847a-dee1-5e90-aa75-e08ca9d39fd8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110495 :question/problem "Given that $3^{2x+3}=5,$ find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Taking $\\log_3$ of both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n   \t3^{2x+3} \t\t\t\t&= 5 \\[3pt]\n\t\\log_3 \\left(3^{2x+3}\\right)\t&= \\log_3 (5) \\[3pt]\n\t2x+3 \t\t\t\t\t&= \\log_3 (5) . \\[3pt]\n}\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx \\approx -0.768\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n**Note:** We used the change of base formula to rewrite the right-hand side, as many calculators can only compute base $10$ logarithms." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110595 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7e1ee8c2-d8a9-5564-8fe2-55ce248be31a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110496 :question/problem "Given that $3\\cdot 6^{x} = 9,$ find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the exponential on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {6}^{x} & =9 \\\\ {6}^{x} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take $\\log_{6}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n   \t6^x \t\t\t\t\t&= 3 \\[3pt]\n\t\\log_6 \\left(6^x\\right)\t&= \\log_6 (3) \\[3pt]\n\tx \t\t\t\t\t&= \\log_6 (3)\\[3pt]\n}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\log(3)}{\\log(6)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx \\approx 0.613\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-4dbd2fd5-279c-5b4f-83af-6acbee35f908" :question/id #uuid "4dbd2fd5-279c-5b4f-83af-6acbee35f908" :question/math-academy-id "q-66801" :question/problem "Find the exact solution of the equation ${3}^{x}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We take ${log}_{3}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & =5 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{x}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx={log}_{3}⁡(5)\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-479444fd-611e-5a03-bcad-55f19ebf647d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-479444fd-611e-5a03-bcad-55f19ebf647d" :answer-field/id #uuid "479444fd-611e-5a03-bcad-55f19ebf647d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cd163f76-39be-503b-8e02-0fc0fc56c0ca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d87bd39e-276b-5919-ae48-a488a12d6528" "answer-cd163f76-39be-503b-8e02-0fc0fc56c0ca" "answer-f4426217-c85d-5fd5-a049-5d1b721018a2" "answer-7f147060-2e3d-530e-8605-1ba13a4114d1" "answer-9dec9e09-3ab9-51d5-93f5-5553f7a2ae1b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d87bd39e-276b-5919-ae48-a488a12d6528" :answer/id #uuid "d87bd39e-276b-5919-ae48-a488a12d6528" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{{log}_{3}⁡(5)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd163f76-39be-503b-8e02-0fc0fc56c0ca" :answer/id #uuid "cd163f76-39be-503b-8e02-0fc0fc56c0ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={log}_{3}⁡(5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4426217-c85d-5fd5-a049-5d1b721018a2" :answer/id #uuid "f4426217-c85d-5fd5-a049-5d1b721018a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f147060-2e3d-530e-8605-1ba13a4114d1" :answer/id #uuid "7f147060-2e3d-530e-8605-1ba13a4114d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={log}_{5}⁡(3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9dec9e09-3ab9-51d5-93f5-5553f7a2ae1b" :answer/id #uuid "9dec9e09-3ab9-51d5-93f5-5553f7a2ae1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-07d6770c-e373-5366-b946-966ce2aa6000" :question/id #uuid "07d6770c-e373-5366-b946-966ce2aa6000" :question/math-academy-id "q-100163" :question/problem "Find the exact solution of the equation ${7}^{x+4}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take ${log}_{7}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{x+4} & =5 \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡({7}^{x+4}) & ={log}_{7}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+4={log}_{7}⁡(5)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & ={log}_{7}⁡(5) \\\\ x & ={log}_{7}⁡(5)-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x={log}_{7}⁡(5)-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3300a13e-fe37-59f5-9d51-81da8e4c45f6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3300a13e-fe37-59f5-9d51-81da8e4c45f6" :answer-field/id #uuid "3300a13e-fe37-59f5-9d51-81da8e4c45f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-49f84153-b03e-5266-a48d-c82f2db5b6aa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-064a3c82-ae53-5db3-ad5c-b3fcfc6fb6ad" "answer-cd3750bd-081d-5764-9284-5e0451f625eb" "answer-7b506411-a799-5138-a14f-4b0c6672b0c9" "answer-49f84153-b03e-5266-a48d-c82f2db5b6aa" "answer-ee944174-45cf-5629-b838-1acad19f5360"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-064a3c82-ae53-5db3-ad5c-b3fcfc6fb6ad" :answer/id #uuid "064a3c82-ae53-5db3-ad5c-b3fcfc6fb6ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={log}_{5}⁡(4)-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd3750bd-081d-5764-9284-5e0451f625eb" :answer/id #uuid "cd3750bd-081d-5764-9284-5e0451f625eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={log}_{7}⁡(5)+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b506411-a799-5138-a14f-4b0c6672b0c9" :answer/id #uuid "7b506411-a799-5138-a14f-4b0c6672b0c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={log}_{7}⁡(5)+7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49f84153-b03e-5266-a48d-c82f2db5b6aa" :answer/id #uuid "49f84153-b03e-5266-a48d-c82f2db5b6aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={log}_{7}⁡(5)-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee944174-45cf-5629-b838-1acad19f5360" :answer/id #uuid "ee944174-45cf-5629-b838-1acad19f5360" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x={log}_{7}⁡(7)-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d436286b-8ea5-5b92-b174-4ebb9b14bf53" :question/id #uuid "d436286b-8ea5-5b92-b174-4ebb9b14bf53" :question/math-academy-id "q-973" :question/problem "Given that ${11}^{4-y}=\\sqrt[4]{3},$ find the value of $y$  rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Taking ${log}_{11}$ of both sides of the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{11}^{4-y} & =\\sqrt[4]{3} \\\\ {log}_{11}⁡({11}^{4-y}) & ={log}_{11}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{3}) \\\\ 4-y & ={log}_{11}⁡({3}^{1/4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n4-y=\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{11}⁡(3)\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and then solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)} \\\\ -y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)}-4 \\\\ y & =4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\ny≈3.885\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f4daa940-75d3-59a9-9409-b1cafb9f426c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f4daa940-75d3-59a9-9409-b1cafb9f426c" :answer-field/id #uuid "f4daa940-75d3-59a9-9409-b1cafb9f426c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-feaf6731-e3b7-599f-a042-7ed2790987cc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-66790320-0c2c-5cc3-b140-997d9491efef" "answer-945201fd-0d4a-5e7f-a3b1-d14b90583f75" "answer-feaf6731-e3b7-599f-a042-7ed2790987cc" "answer-26a853f4-f0a8-5a09-b9c0-56c7fc01358d" "answer-10304769-0b45-5ddb-960a-5561ac8890cf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66790320-0c2c-5cc3-b140-997d9491efef" :answer/id #uuid "66790320-0c2c-5cc3-b140-997d9491efef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2.981" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-945201fd-0d4a-5e7f-a3b1-d14b90583f75" :answer/id #uuid "945201fd-0d4a-5e7f-a3b1-d14b90583f75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3.454" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-feaf6731-e3b7-599f-a042-7ed2790987cc" :answer/id #uuid "feaf6731-e3b7-599f-a042-7ed2790987cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.885" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26a853f4-f0a8-5a09-b9c0-56c7fc01358d" :answer/id #uuid "26a853f4-f0a8-5a09-b9c0-56c7fc01358d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.115" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10304769-0b45-5ddb-960a-5561ac8890cf" :answer/id #uuid "10304769-0b45-5ddb-960a-5561ac8890cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.141" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-918c847a-dee1-5e90-aa75-e08ca9d39fd8" :question/id #uuid "918c847a-dee1-5e90-aa75-e08ca9d39fd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-66811" :question/problem "Given that ${3}^{4-2x}=\\sqrt{2},$ find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Taking ${log}_{3}$ of both sides of the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4-2x} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{4-2x}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ 4-2x & ={log}_{3}⁡({2}^{1/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n4-2x=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(2)\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-2x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ -2x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}-4 \\\\ x & =2-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈1.842\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fb33b195-f963-5860-8754-612425a1f549"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fb33b195-f963-5860-8754-612425a1f549" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb33b195-f963-5860-8754-612425a1f549" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d6441217-85f1-5384-af07-4fb4353c7e2c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ddefe1b3-5955-5cb7-9393-02639a571285" "answer-ca20cb11-444e-5551-a283-109595f4bfc0" "answer-78981f9f-c8a0-501c-a93c-1f3d6d8a9843" "answer-21a7be4c-c258-5104-8444-2a5ac6bf1461" "answer-d6441217-85f1-5384-af07-4fb4353c7e2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddefe1b3-5955-5cb7-9393-02639a571285" :answer/id #uuid "ddefe1b3-5955-5cb7-9393-02639a571285" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1.604" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca20cb11-444e-5551-a283-109595f4bfc0" :answer/id #uuid "ca20cb11-444e-5551-a283-109595f4bfc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1.842" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78981f9f-c8a0-501c-a93c-1f3d6d8a9843" :answer/id #uuid "78981f9f-c8a0-501c-a93c-1f3d6d8a9843" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.604" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21a7be4c-c258-5104-8444-2a5ac6bf1461" :answer/id #uuid "21a7be4c-c258-5104-8444-2a5ac6bf1461" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.685" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6441217-85f1-5384-af07-4fb4353c7e2c" :answer/id #uuid "d6441217-85f1-5384-af07-4fb4353c7e2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.842" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186234211 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({4}^{y})-14 & =19 \\\\ 3({4}^{y}) & =33 \\\\ {4}^{y} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take ${log}_{4}$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({4}^{y}) & ={log}_{4}⁡(11) \\\\ y & ={log}_{4}⁡(11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y={log}_{4}⁡(11).$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f6a67648-6807-50b6-a6d7-45ce416912c3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f6a67648-6807-50b6-a6d7-45ce416912c3" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6a67648-6807-50b6-a6d7-45ce416912c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ea9486c9-bf88-5eab-bce1-b8a6a8aa9b65" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e0f5a6cf-f068-59be-af8a-466cd2c91f78" "answer-82826981-110c-5a1a-858e-43f3412d0ed5" "answer-ea9486c9-bf88-5eab-bce1-b8a6a8aa9b65" "answer-9de9a0bc-28a2-5c16-8937-334d75654754" "answer-dcc73e5b-9e38-59e8-b84c-76a4fa387198"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0f5a6cf-f068-59be-af8a-466cd2c91f78" :answer/id #uuid "e0f5a6cf-f068-59be-af8a-466cd2c91f78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{log}_{4}⁡(11)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82826981-110c-5a1a-858e-43f3412d0ed5" :answer/id #uuid "82826981-110c-5a1a-858e-43f3412d0ed5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{{log}_{4}⁡(3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea9486c9-bf88-5eab-bce1-b8a6a8aa9b65" :answer/id #uuid "ea9486c9-bf88-5eab-bce1-b8a6a8aa9b65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={log}_{4}⁡(11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9de9a0bc-28a2-5c16-8937-334d75654754" :answer/id #uuid "9de9a0bc-28a2-5c16-8937-334d75654754" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={log}_{4}⁡(33)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcc73e5b-9e38-59e8-b84c-76a4fa387198" :answer/id #uuid "dcc73e5b-9e38-59e8-b84c-76a4fa387198" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={log}_{4}⁡(5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7e1ee8c2-d8a9-5564-8fe2-55ce248be31a" :question/id #uuid "7e1ee8c2-d8a9-5564-8fe2-55ce248be31a" :question/math-academy-id "q-31891" :question/problem "Given that ${5}^{2a+3}-13=25,$ find the value of $a$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2a+3}-13 & =25 \\\\ {5}^{2a+3} & =38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take ${log}_{5}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2a+3} & =38 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{2a+3}) & ={log}_{5}⁡(38) \\\\ 2a+3 & ={log}_{5}⁡(38)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+3 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)} \\\\ 2a & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}-3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\na≈-0.370\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-51e41568-3547-5b0e-b361-b42d6abf90f3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-51e41568-3547-5b0e-b361-b42d6abf90f3" :answer-field/id #uuid "51e41568-3547-5b0e-b361-b42d6abf90f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3755bb99-c614-5cf1-80fc-6be88288dbde" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3755bb99-c614-5cf1-80fc-6be88288dbde" "answer-b73616bd-6754-5514-aa8a-eead41959119" "answer-249cec14-666a-5fae-9615-1544a0eec215" "answer-f6221a2d-763a-5c87-bb34-2b68cf8fa1c6" "answer-767e7c4e-ad51-5769-a990-6385b5908531"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3755bb99-c614-5cf1-80fc-6be88288dbde" :answer/id #uuid "3755bb99-c614-5cf1-80fc-6be88288dbde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.370" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b73616bd-6754-5514-aa8a-eead41959119" :answer/id #uuid "b73616bd-6754-5514-aa8a-eead41959119" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.740" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-249cec14-666a-5fae-9615-1544a0eec215" :answer/id #uuid "249cec14-666a-5fae-9615-1544a0eec215" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.370" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6221a2d-763a-5c87-bb34-2b68cf8fa1c6" :answer/id #uuid "f6221a2d-763a-5c87-bb34-2b68cf8fa1c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.740" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-767e7c4e-ad51-5769-a990-6385b5908531" :answer/id #uuid "767e7c4e-ad51-5769-a990-6385b5908531" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.260" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111879 :tutorial/content "We can use logarithms to solve equations where the variable is in an exponent.\nFor instance, suppose we are given the equation \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}^x=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that the base of the exponential is ${\\color{blue}2}.$ So, if we take $\\log_{\\color{blue}2}$ of both sides of the equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the fact that $\\log_{\\color{blue}b} \\left( {\\color{blue}b}^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\log_{\\color{blue}2} (5).\n$$\n\nThis is the solution! We often present the solution of an exponential equation as an exact solution in logarithmic form.\n\nNote that when taking the $\\log_b$ of both sides of an exponential equation, we always use the base of the exponential as the base of the logarithm." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110848 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186126038 :answer-field/choices 17592186126039] [:db/retract 17592186126038 :answer-field/choices 17592186126040] [:db/retract 17592186126038 :answer-field/choices 17592186126042] [:db/retract 17592186126038 :answer-field/choices 17592186126043] [:db/retract 17592186129855 :question/answer-fields 17592186128611] {:db/id 17592186129221 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-60f150de-1cdb-534e-a48d-461dca7bdb52"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128994 :question/problem "Let $R = [-1,4]\\times [1,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that $m_{ij} = M_{ij} = 3$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and $m_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion $R_{ij}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129220 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ac9755ca-22ae-5d63-a731-5137b2daeebf" "question-3c4eccd2-7dd0-53ca-8b67-e044fcc594a5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-60f150de-1cdb-534e-a48d-461dca7bdb52" :question/id #uuid "60f150de-1cdb-534e-a48d-461dca7bdb52" :question/math-academy-id "q-123747" :question/problem "Let $R=[2,4]\\times [3,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=4y-2x+3.$ Further, let \n\n$$\nI={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nbe the unique number such that over all rectangular partitions $P$ of $R,$ we have \n$L(f,P)\\,\\le I\\,\\le U(f,P),$\nwhere $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P)$ are the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums, and \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhat expression is missing from the double summation above?" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe need to compute $L(f,P).$ \n\nNotice that for all subregions ${R}_{ij},$ we have that\n\n- $f(x,y)=4y-2x+3$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=4y-2x+3$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom right corner* of each subregion. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}=f({x}_{i},{y}_{j-1})=4{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+3\n$$\n\nand the lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(4{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+3)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $4{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bee740fc-a270-5867-b772-361b786a0c9e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bee740fc-a270-5867-b772-361b786a0c9e" :answer-field/id #uuid "bee740fc-a270-5867-b772-361b786a0c9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1c368a8e-c366-5a8d-8ec4-9ead969cd6ca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b58dea12-732d-547a-a287-d9c96084a797" "answer-1c368a8e-c366-5a8d-8ec4-9ead969cd6ca" "answer-6dda395a-03b9-501e-8d34-5bef5d0b9b01" "answer-6f849b3f-61a5-5fd9-8b65-3d0c1043b4ed" "answer-ceb9fe62-b9a6-537f-b7d5-30ced159b3fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b58dea12-732d-547a-a287-d9c96084a797" :answer/id #uuid "b58dea12-732d-547a-a287-d9c96084a797" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i-1}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c368a8e-c366-5a8d-8ec4-9ead969cd6ca" :answer/id #uuid "1c368a8e-c366-5a8d-8ec4-9ead969cd6ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6dda395a-03b9-501e-8d34-5bef5d0b9b01" :answer/id #uuid "6dda395a-03b9-501e-8d34-5bef5d0b9b01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{y}_{j}-2{x}_{i-1}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f849b3f-61a5-5fd9-8b65-3d0c1043b4ed" :answer/id #uuid "6f849b3f-61a5-5fd9-8b65-3d0c1043b4ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{y}_{j}-2{x}_{i}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ceb9fe62-b9a6-537f-b7d5-30ced159b3fe" :answer/id #uuid "ceb9fe62-b9a6-537f-b7d5-30ced159b3fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the options is correct" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129423 :question/problem "Let $R=[0,1]\\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x-y+2.$ Further, let \n\n$$\nI={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nbe the unique number such that over all rectangular partitions $P$ of $R,$ we have \n$L(f,P)\\,\\le I\\,\\le U(f,P),$\nwhere $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P)$ are the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums, and \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhat expression is missing from the double summation above?" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe need to compute $L(f,P).$ \n\nNotice that for all subregions ${R}_{ij},$ we have that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x-y+2$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x-y+2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top left corner* of each subregion. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}=f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j})={x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j}+2\n$$\n\nand the lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j}+2)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is ${x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j}+2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186126038 :answer-field/correct "answer-50eef5ee-6542-542a-9127-74ff6e5db4df" :answer-field/choices ["answer-27d7c729-1758-5458-b490-f386514b070a" "answer-50eef5ee-6542-542a-9127-74ff6e5db4df" "answer-d1b7d003-ef6e-507b-9256-ab33fe41d489" "answer-d4834f78-0535-5410-999e-445c3c8d2009"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27d7c729-1758-5458-b490-f386514b070a" :answer/id #uuid "27d7c729-1758-5458-b490-f386514b070a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j-1}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50eef5ee-6542-542a-9127-74ff6e5db4df" :answer/id #uuid "50eef5ee-6542-542a-9127-74ff6e5db4df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1b7d003-ef6e-507b-9256-ab33fe41d489" :answer/id #uuid "d1b7d003-ef6e-507b-9256-ab33fe41d489" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}_{i}-{y}_{j-1}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4834f78-0535-5410-999e-445c3c8d2009" :answer/id #uuid "d4834f78-0535-5410-999e-445c3c8d2009" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}_{i}-{y}_{j}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129855 :question/problem "Let $R=[-5,-2]\\times [-6,-1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=3$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186259569 :question/problem "Let $R=[-5,-2]\\times [3,7]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=1$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186259568 :question/problem "Let $R=[-1,3]\\times [2,5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=-3$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186341844 :question/problem "Let $R=[0,4]\\times [2,6]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=4$ for all partition $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186259570 :question/problem "Let $R=[1,7]\\times [4,6]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=6$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ac9755ca-22ae-5d63-a731-5137b2daeebf" :question/id #uuid "ac9755ca-22ae-5d63-a731-5137b2daeebf" :question/math-academy-id "q-123850" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [2,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(8-2{x}_{i}-{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(8-2{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0}) \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}-{x}_{0})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that ${\\overset{¯}{x}}_{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})$ will always lie between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$, and similarly ${\\overset{¯}{y}}_{j}=\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})$ will always lie between ${y}_{j-1}$ and ${y}_{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n8-2{x}_{i}-{y}_{j}\\le 8-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})\\le 8-2{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j-1}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to \n\n$$\n8-2{x}_{i}-{y}_{j}\\le 8-({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})\\le 8-2{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j-1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}$ and applying the double summation gives:\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[8-({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})]Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & \\le 8{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,-\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\le U(f,P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})(3-2)=4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{1}{2}({3}^{2}-{2}^{2})(2-0)=5 \\\\ 8{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =8(2-0)(3-2)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le 16-4-5\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le 7\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-01076285-ae72-5758-9587-05c045ac247c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-01076285-ae72-5758-9587-05c045ac247c" :answer-field/id #uuid "01076285-ae72-5758-9587-05c045ac247c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8fe09f3e-2c96-5c5e-a391-663478d871a7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cab1b35e-19f6-572c-9e9a-2c6be63ba9dc" "answer-619565ed-3d57-5036-95fa-ba9547f31f6e" "answer-99233c84-4925-5f97-8b2b-7a305a74c813" "answer-8fe09f3e-2c96-5c5e-a391-663478d871a7" "answer-309eed89-dce7-588a-b072-9c591d942b77"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cab1b35e-19f6-572c-9e9a-2c6be63ba9dc" :answer/id #uuid "cab1b35e-19f6-572c-9e9a-2c6be63ba9dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-619565ed-3d57-5036-95fa-ba9547f31f6e" :answer/id #uuid "619565ed-3d57-5036-95fa-ba9547f31f6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99233c84-4925-5f97-8b2b-7a305a74c813" :answer/id #uuid "99233c84-4925-5f97-8b2b-7a305a74c813" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fe09f3e-2c96-5c5e-a391-663478d871a7" :answer/id #uuid "8fe09f3e-2c96-5c5e-a391-663478d871a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-309eed89-dce7-588a-b072-9c591d942b77" :answer/id #uuid "309eed89-dce7-588a-b072-9c591d942b77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3c4eccd2-7dd0-53ca-8b67-e044fcc594a5" :question/id #uuid "3c4eccd2-7dd0-53ca-8b67-e044fcc594a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-123856" :question/problem "Let $R=[-3,1]\\times [2,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}-{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}-{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that ${\\overset{¯}{x}}_{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})$ will always lie between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$, and similarly ${\\overset{¯}{y}}_{j}=\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})$ will always lie between ${y}_{j-1}$ and ${y}_{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}_{j-1}-{x}_{i}\\le \\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})\\le {y}_{j}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}$ and applying the double summation gives:\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})]Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & \\le \\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}-\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\le U(f,P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{j-1}+{x}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{1}{2}({1}^{2}-(-3)^{2})(4-2)=-8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{1}{2}({4}^{2}-{2}^{2})(1-(-3))=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le 24-(-8)\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le 32\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=32.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d3b13d40-42f8-5fcf-b562-1fbe4a131420"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d3b13d40-42f8-5fcf-b562-1fbe4a131420" :answer-field/id #uuid "d3b13d40-42f8-5fcf-b562-1fbe4a131420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d209e16c-aeb8-54b1-b37c-3084d1b1f440" :answer-field/choices ["answer-184012f0-2b52-515a-b494-8bc567e92f28" "answer-11660d74-dd0a-5819-9d4e-b50594a56164" "answer-5327fde3-a474-599f-919c-4b4af0d58991" "answer-662a97ed-0348-5971-99ff-f6ffd17244cb" "answer-d209e16c-aeb8-54b1-b37c-3084d1b1f440"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-184012f0-2b52-515a-b494-8bc567e92f28" :answer/id #uuid "184012f0-2b52-515a-b494-8bc567e92f28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11660d74-dd0a-5819-9d4e-b50594a56164" :answer/id #uuid "11660d74-dd0a-5819-9d4e-b50594a56164" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5327fde3-a474-599f-919c-4b4af0d58991" :answer/id #uuid "5327fde3-a474-599f-919c-4b4af0d58991" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-662a97ed-0348-5971-99ff-f6ffd17244cb" :answer/id #uuid "662a97ed-0348-5971-99ff-f6ffd17244cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d209e16c-aeb8-54b1-b37c-3084d1b1f440" :answer/id #uuid "d209e16c-aeb8-54b1-b37c-3084d1b1f440" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130379 :tutorial/content "Let $f(x,y)$ be a positive, continuous function defined on a closed and bounded rectangle $R,$ and let $P = P_1\\times P_2$ be a partition of $R.$ We know that the volume $V$ bounded by the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the region $R$ in the $xy$-plane satisfies\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq V \\leq U(f,P),\n$$\n\nwhere $L(f, P)$ and $U(f, P)$ are the lower and upper Riemann sums of $f$ with respect to $P.$\n\nImagine that our initial partition $P$ consists of a few rectangles only. Let's visualize the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums, as shown below:\n\n![](images/89/89ad0fd33f34cfad4f55fc2085360878460df92479f5f938ecd254420457f3f4.png)\n\n \n\nThen, we refine $P$ by adding more points to $P_1$ and $P_2,$ resulting in more rectangles in $P{:}$ \n\n![](images/56/5635f58e19c47d6a9cfeab49c298ce5ca7aa1d8697e1b53390bb3bc31859637c.png)\n\n \n\nRefining the partition once again, we get the following:\n\n![](images/b2/b22f267ecf28627404eb90928e44d59b2873e88adc22ffaaa0a1a3335fffa307.png)\n\n \n\nAs we continue to refine $P$ by adding more and more points to $P_1$ and $P_2,$ we find that the values of the lower and upper Riemann sums approach the volume $V{:}$\n\n- $L(f, P)\\to V$ from below, and\n\n- $U(f, P) \\to V$ from above.\n\nWe define the **double integral** of $f(x,y)$ over $R$ as the *unique* number $I$ that satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nfor *all* partitions $P$ of $R,$ and we write\n\n$$\nI=\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe double integral gives the exact volume bounded between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and $R.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129337 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
